 they struck oil  themselves no it was right here because  this marked the beginning of Abu Dhabi  extending significant financial support  to Dubai that's still continuing to this  day  foreign  ever since the creation of the UAE Abu  Dhabi has acted as the Godfather of  Dubai funding dozens of projects handing  out several loans and bailing them out  on numerous occasions one of the first  projects that Dubai embarked on after  teaming up with Abu Dhabi was building a  new Port called the port of jabel Ali  this port was built throughout the late  1970s and it would be opened in February  of 1979. since then this port has become  home to 5 000 different companies from  120 different countries and the port of  jabel Ali currently ranks as the 11th  busiest port in the world but as you  might remember this wasn't Dubai's first  shot at the building a port in fact they  tried building a tax report way back in  1901 that however was wiped out by a  single storm while the port of jabel Ali  has survived numerous explosions attacks  and natural disasters the key difference  between the two is that one was backed  by oil money while the other was not  anyway shortly after the support was  completed Dubai set out on her biggest  and most successful project of all time  Emirates Airlines Emirates was  personally funded by the royal family of  Dubai in 1985 and Essence turned into  one of the most luxurious and respected  airlines in the world but while people  view Emirates as being rich with their  jumbo a380s Emirates actually makes less  money than Delta American Airlines  Lufthansa United and even Southwest so  Emirates is much more of an image piece  than a true profit driver and how was  this image piece funded well I think you  know the answer moving on we have all of  a Dubai's vanity projects first they had  the Burj all Arab in 1999 then they had  the Palm Islands in 2004. after that it  was Atlantis in 2008 followed up by the  Burj Khalifa in 2010 and now we have the  quick Tower though that may be canceled  as the reports Airlines and vacation  destinations grew in popularity these  sectors of the economy simply a how to  paste the relatively small oil industry  and this is why Dubai is able to claim  that oil only makes up one percent of  their GDP but guess who comes to their  rescue whenever they need help well it's  of course their average Partners Abu  Dhabi a perfect example of this is 2008  during the great financial recession as  the global economy cooled down it's hard  to travel and the revenue that Dubai  pulled in they were no longer able to  keep up with vanity projects like the  Burj Khalifa but luckily for them Abu  Dhabi would come to the rescue with the  10 billion dollar bailout we should also  mention that Dubai has yet to pay back  Abu Dhabi not that Abu Dhabi  particularly cares Dubai has  consistently just been rolling over the  dead ever since the financial crisis and  the currently or Abu Dhabi 20 billion  dollars and that's just their debt to  Abu Dhabi Dubai also has loads of  internal and international debt Moody's  and the s p estimate that Dubai total  debt load stands at 79 in dollars  clearly regardless of whether Dubai is  oil dependent or not there's no question  that they're debt dependent  in the end while Dubai likes to convince  the world is that they're completely  self-sufficient and that they've been  able to beat their oil dependents this  is simply not the case even though oil  does not account for a large portion of  their GDP it's literally the only reason  the country was able to develop in the  first place and it's the only reason  that they remain solvent today if it  wasn't for Abu dhabi's oil bailout Dubai  would have likely went to bankrupt back  in 2009 and it's not clear if the Burj  Khalifa would have even been opened this  is probably why they decided to name the  building after the ruler of Dubai at the  time at Khalifa bin zayeda on ION the  truth is that Dubai isn't